   \end{array}\right)=\sigma h  \left(\begin{array}{c}
   \varphi_0'  +h p_1  \\ h p_2
   \end{array}\right).
   \end{equation}
   To the leading order $h$, we have the equations
   \begin{eqnarray*}
   	& & 	L_+ p_1 - 12 \varphi_0 (a_0 \Psi_1^0+b_0 \Psi_2^0) \varphi_0'=\sigma \varphi_0' \\
   	& & L_- p_2 + 2 \varphi_0(2 b_0\Psi_1^0 - 2 a_0 \Psi_2^0-D_2^0) \varphi_0'=0
   \end{eqnarray*}
   The second equation  always has a solution as $2 \varphi_0(2 b_0\Psi_1^0 - 2 a_0 \Psi_2^0-D_2^0) \varphi_0'$ is an odd function, so it is perpendicular to $Ker[L_-]=span[\varphi_0]$.

   The first equation requires  the solvability condition $\sigma \varphi_0' + 12 \varphi_0 (a_0 \Psi_1^0+b_0 \Psi_2^0) \varphi_0'\perp \varphi_0'$. Noting that $a_0 \Psi_1^0+b_0 \Psi_2^0=a_0 L_+^{-1}[1]$,   we derive the formula for $\sigma_0$,
   \begin{equation} \sigma_0=-12 \f{\dpr{\varphi_0 \varphi_0'(a_0 \Psi_1^0+b_0 \Psi_2^0)}{\varphi_0'}}{\|\varphi_0'\|^2}=
   -12 \f{a_0}{\|\varphi_0'\|^2} \dpr{ L_{+}^{-1}[1]}{\varphi_0 (\varphi_0')^2}.
   \end{equation}
   This quantity can be computed explicitly, in fact  $\dpr{ L_{+}^{-1}[1]}{\varphi_0 (\varphi_0')^2}=0$, see Proposition \ref{antany} below. Our analysis is not precise enough\footnote{we do not have the precise asymptotic expressions of order  $O(h^2)$ above, although this is in principle  possible, after heavy computations.} to compute explicitly the next order, $O(h^2)$ term.

   All this means is that the translational eigenvalue for ${\mathcal L}_{+,h}$  is zero to first order in $h$. As we have shown above, with index counting calculations, it turns out that this eigenvalue must have moved to the left (of order $O(h^2)$) to become a negative one.

